ExoPlayer / Media3 — what it actually is
----------------------------------------
ExoPlayer is Google's open-source media player for Android. (Its modern name is
"Media3"; the `androidx.media3` libraries are the current home of ExoPlayer.)
It is *not* a media app — it is a playback engine/library you embed, the same
way AntennaPod, Podcast Addict, NewPipe, and VLC-on-Android embed it. Today our
app uses the OS's built-in `android.media.MediaPlayer` to play each rendered
WAV; ExoPlayer is the heavier, more capable alternative that sits in the same
slot.

What it is intended for / what it does that matters to us
---------------------------------------------------------
- Precise, sample-accurate seeking inside a media file. This is the big one for
  pre-render: "skip to the next paragraph" becomes `player.seekTo(cueOffsetMs)`
  — instant and gapless — instead of today's "stop and re-render from an index."
- It reads media through the same platform `MediaExtractor`/`MediaCodec` stack,
  so it plays AAC-in-M4A natively with correct timestamps (including the
  encoder-delay "edit list", which is where naive position math drifts).
- It is built around a `Player` interface with a listener that reports
  position/state; you attach a `MediaSession` (and a `MediaSessionService`) and
  get the lock-screen + notification + Bluetooth transport for free, with a
  real seek bar and buffered position. This is the "podcast-like" end state
  ticket 09 flagged.
- Speed control, gapless concatenation, and per-media-item playlists fall out of
  the same API (not needed for v1, but free later).

How big it is
-------------
It is the single largest dependency we would add. The core `media3-exoplayer`
library plus its `media3-common` / `media3-datasource` companions adds on the
order of ~2–4 MB to the APK (more if we also adopt `media3-session` for the
`MediaSessionService`, and `media3-ui` for a canned player view — which we would
NOT, since our UI is Compose). This is a real, measurable cost against the
"small-ish APK" goal, and it is the main reason I am not recommending we pull it
in for v1 (below). If it ever matters, the exact delta is a one-line build away
from being measured.

Benefits (adopting ExoPlayer/Media3 for the pre-rendered path)
--------------------------------------------------------------
- True skip-forward/back by paragraph (seek to cue offset) — fixes the one thing
  you flagged as still-rough in current playback.
- Robust background playback + a proper podcast-style notification with seek.
- One engine to play any future offline file; the current per-WAV
  `MediaPlayer` "dance" in SpeechController could eventually be retired.

Costs / risks
-------------
- APK size (~2–4 MB), against our "keep it minimal" goal.
- A second playback path to maintain (live TTS vs. pre-rendered file), plus
  learning a new, fairly large API.
- Migration friction: our `WcastMediaService` currently hand-rolls a framework
  `MediaSession`; the clean Media3 way replaces some of that with
  `MediaSessionService`.

What this means for existing functionality (the part you asked about)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Nothing about today's behavior changes. Pre-render is an *additive* feature:

- Live TTS playback (`SpeechController`: synthesizeToFile -> WAV -> framework
  `MediaPlayer`, with the ticket-02 look-ahead) stays exactly as it is. You can
  still open any article and press play with no pre-render involved.
- Pre-rendered playback is a *separate, opt-in* path that only activates when a
  bookmarked article has a finished `.m4a`. Tapping the article then plays the
  baked file instead of re-synthesizing. If no `.m4a` exists, you get today's
  live path — no change.
- So "current functionality seems fine" is preserved; pre-render is purely a
  nicer, offline, no-gap alternative for articles you explicitly rendered.

Recommendation (the either/or you asked me to weigh)
----------------------------------------------------
Ship pre-render in two layers, and do NOT take on ExoPlayer yet:

1. Build the pipeline + single `.m4a` + cue table now, and play it with the
   *framework* `MediaPlayer` we already use (poll `getCurrentPosition()` ~every
   250 ms, map it to the cue table, and feed the same "active paragraph" signal
   the left margin/scroll already consume). Zero new dependencies.
2. Keep ExoPlayer/Media3 as its own, separately-scoped ticket (the "Media3
   step" ticket 09 already queued). If you later want true skip-by-paragraph and
   a podcast-style seek bar on pre-rendered files, adopt it *just* for that
   path.

This honors "minimal dependencies / small-ish APK," gives you the offline
feature now, and defers the only dependency question worth agonizing over.

Why the cue table still works with framework MediaPlayer (and the one risk)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
We control the timestamps when we encode+mux, so the cue table is computed from
cumulative PCM sample counts per paragraph — not guessed from wall time. The one
thing to validate on-device: framework `MediaPlayer`'s reported position on
AAC/M4A can carry a small constant offset at the very start (encoder priming).
If it shows up, we add one measured correction constant to the cue lookup. This
is exactly the kind of drift ExoPlayer handles for us automatically — which is
why it stays on the table as the "polish" step.

